  "worked_solution": "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,-8)$ and passes through the point $(5,10).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,-8)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(5,10),$ we substitute $x=5$ and $y=10$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5+1)^{2} & =4p(10+8) \\\\ 36 & =4p(18) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =2(y+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =2(0+8) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ x & =\\pm 4-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-5$  and $3.$",
